\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The estimate is right before it is confident, and says so.} The same room, from one closed-loop run on a real LiDAR model. The pale band is ground truth and each dimension carries its measured value and signed error: \SI {5}{\milli \meter } on a \SI {6}{\meter } wall is \SI {0.08}{\percent }, too fine to draw at any scale that also fits the room, so the agreement is shown and the discrepancy is stated. Both panels are drawn with the long axis horizontal; the map frame is gauge-free and the re-anchor rotates it between the two, but every quantity here---edge lengths, corner $\sigma $, the comparison with truth---is invariant to that rotation, so what differs between the panels is only the uncertainty. \emph  {Left}, after \SI {681}{\second } parked: the geometry is already within \SI {15}{\milli \meter } of truth, yet every corner declares almost a metre of uncertainty and every edge sits at its prior, so the layout is withheld. Standing still re-observes the same walls from the same viewpoint, and the information those observations carry saturates. \emph  {Right}, after a single rotation in place: the same geometry, now with corner $\sigma $ smaller by a factor of \num {32} and every corner under the publication gate. The estimator does not earn precision by waiting, and does not claim any.}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{1}{\textbf {The estimate is right before it is confident, and says so.} The same room, from one closed-loop run on a real LiDAR model. The pale band is ground truth and each dimension carries its measured value and signed error: \SI {5}{\milli \meter } on a \SI {6}{\meter } wall is \SI {0.08}{\percent }, too fine to draw at any scale that also fits the room, so the agreement is shown and the discrepancy is stated. Both panels are drawn with the long axis horizontal; the map frame is gauge-free and the re-anchor rotates it between the two, but every quantity here---edge lengths, corner $\sigma $, the comparison with truth---is invariant to that rotation, so what differs between the panels is only the uncertainty. \emph {Left}, after \SI {681}{\second } parked: the geometry is already within \SI {15}{\milli \meter } of truth, yet every corner declares almost a metre of uncertainty and every edge sits at its prior, so the layout is withheld. Standing still re-observes the same walls from the same viewpoint, and the information those observations carry saturates. \emph {Right}, after a single rotation in place: the same geometry, now with corner $\sigma $ smaller by a factor of \num {32} and every corner under the publication gate. The estimator does not earn precision by waiting, and does not claim any}{figure.1}{}}
